# tab-indent.tflw — CHECK-ONLY FIXTURE (M98c): a file indented with tabs — demonstrates `tflw
# check`’s teaching diagnostic (TF048: lexer, tflw M98c / review `A1-12`/`A1-13`).
#
# Split out of `TF003`, which was carrying this *and* "indentation does not line up with any
# enclosing block" under one code while documenting only the second. That row is what SPEC §17, the
# docs-site Reference page and LSP hover are all generated from, so one code meaning two things made
# four surfaces wrong at once — and the two conditions have unrelated repairs: re-indent a block, or
# change an editor setting.
#
# Reported **once per file**, at the first offending line, with the number of remaining lines in the
# help. The rule fired once per *line* before, so one wrong editor setting produced 100 identical
# errors on a 100-line file — none of them a separate mistake, all of them with the same one-setting
# fix. The rule itself is unchanged; M98c is also where it was first written down in `GRAMMAR.md`
# § Lexical, having never appeared there.
#
# Every body line below is indented with a literal tab, so the count in the `= help:` line is the
# part to read.
#
# Run explicitly (dot-directory, invalid syntax — see bad-keyword.tflw’s header for why):
#   npx tflw check tests/.checkonly/tab-indent.tflw

test "a body indented with tabs instead of spaces"
	api GET /health
	expect status equals 200

test "a second tab-indented test, so the help line has more than zero to count"
	api GET /products
	expect status equals 200
